MEASURED STARTING POINT (not an estimate -- ngspice, tt corner, VDD=1.8, vcm=0.9, CL=10p)
  Two distinct seeds exist and they are NOT the same circuit. Both are recorded
  because reading a delta against the wrong one is a wasted iteration.

  (A) FILED netlist  netlist/two_stage_rz.sp, as design-sheets-checker ran it
        Gain 32.2936 dB · UGBW 11.4564 MHz · PM 70.6228 deg · Power 0.585366 mW
  (B) TEMPLATE seed  sizing/two_stage_rz_tuning.sp, the loop's iteration-0 state
        Differs from (A) by the tie-group re-expression ONLY: the nfet bias
        family is re-expressed on one shared unit device w=47.2, m=5/2/7, which
        holds XMN4 at 236.0 um but moves XMN3 102.6 -> 94.4 um (-8.0%) and
        XMN5 345.0 -> 330.4 um (-4.2%). Less tail current and less stage-2 load
        current, so (B) starts SLOWER than (A) -- expected, printed by Step 1 as
        `<-- seed moved this leg`, and covered by open_questions Q1.
  THE LOOP TUNES (B). Deltas are measured from (B).

THE TWO BARS
  real   : spec/target_spec.json
  harder : sizing/harder_target_spec.json  (+10%; a CEILING gets MORE allowance,
           a RANGE closes inward by 10% of its width)

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
KEY 1 -- Gain          FLOOR 40 dB          harder 44 dB
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
DIRECTION   FLOOR: met when sim >= 40 dB. Higher is better, no upper limit.
EXPRESSION  Av = [gm1 * (ro_XMN1 || ro_XMP2)] * [gm2 * (ro_XMP3 || ro_XMN5)]
            -- two cascaded stages, no cascode anywhere, so total gain is the
            product of two plain gm*ro terms and nothing lifts either one.
INSTANCES   gm1: XMN1/XMN2 (MN1_W, MN1_L, MN1_M) biased by tail XMN3 (MN3_M)
            ro1: XMP1/XMP2 (MP1_L dominates) in parallel with XMN1/XMN2's ro
            gm2: XMP3 (MP3_W, MP3_L)
            ro2: XMP3 (MP3_L) in parallel with XMN5 (MN4_L, shared)
GAP         seed (B) is BELOW (A)'s 32.2936 dB; needs >= 40, i.e. >= +7.7 dB
            = a factor 2.43 in volts. Large but not exotic.
PLAUSIBILITY  PLAUSIBLE -- and the reason is specific, not optimism: EVERY MOS
            in this netlist is at l = 0.15 um, which is sky130's minimum. That
            is the WORST intrinsic gain (gm/gds) the process offers, on every
            device at once. Raising l on the load devices (MP1_L, MN4_L, MP3_L)
            is the direct, entirely untried lever, and it costs current nothing.
            Corroboration: the same topology and PDK reached 51.97 dB in
            test_miller_ota_0907 -- context only, since that netlist's mirror
            was invalid, but it bounds the topology's capability from below.
WATCH       gm/gds per device, not the dB alone. If gm/gds has saturated at the
            l in force, more l is the answer; if it has not, the shortfall is
            loading and the speed keys will show it.
TENSION     l up -> ro up (Gain up) but gm down at fixed current (UGBW down) and
            Cgg up (poles down, PM down). Gain is bought from UGBW here.
SIMULATE    .ac dec 10 1 10G ; SAVE v(vout). Gain = |v(vout)| in dB at the first
            swept point (the registered `ac` extractor's mag[0]).
SAVE ALSO   gm, gds, id, vds, vgs, vdsat, cgg on XMN1, XMP2, XMP3, XMN5 (the
            four devices in the expression). The op-probe splice provides these.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
KEY 2 -- UGBW          FLOOR 15 MHz         harder 16.5 MHz
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
DIRECTION   FLOOR: met when sim >= 15 MHz.
EXPRESSION  UGBW = gm1 / (2*pi*Cc), with Cc = XC0 area x ~2 fF/um^2
            (45 x 45 um -> ~4.05 pF as written).
INSTANCES   gm1: XMN1/XMN2 (MN1_W, MN1_L, MN1_M), whose bias current is
            (MN3_M / MN4_M) x I_ref -- so MN3_M and MN4_W move it too.
            Cc: XC0 (C0_W, C0_L) -- pure geometry, costs no current at all.
GAP         (A) measured 11.4564 MHz; (B) starts lower still (less tail current).
            Needs >= 15 MHz: about +31% on (A), more on (B).
PLAUSIBILITY  PLAUSIBLE, and this is the key with the cleanest lever in the whole
            design: UGBW is inversely proportional to Cc, and Cc is set by two
            tunable geometry variables (C0_W, C0_L) that draw zero current.
            Shrinking the 45x45 cap is a first-order, immediate move.
TENSION     Cc down -> UGBW up but pole splitting weakens -> PM down. Since PM
            starts ABOVE its range (see KEY 3) that trade is in our favour here,
            which is the single most important fact in this analysis.
SIMULATE    Same .ac run and same saved node. UGBW = the 0 dB crossing, log-
            interpolated by the registered extractor. If the response never
            crosses 0 dB inside 1 Hz..10 GHz the extractor returns None -- that
            is "not measured", never 0.
SAVE ALSO   gm and id on XMN1/XMN2 and XMN3 (gm1 and the tail current that sets
            it); cgg on XMP3 (which loads net5 alongside Cc).

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
KEY 3 -- PM            RANGE [50, 70] deg   harder [52, 68] deg
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
DIRECTION   RANGE: met when 50 <= sim <= 70. IT CAN FAIL FROM ABOVE. This is the
            one key in this spec that a "more is better" reflex gets wrong.
EXPRESSION  Dominant pole p1 ~ 1/(gm2*ro1*ro2*Cc); output pole p2 ~ gm2/CL with
            CL = 10 pF fixed by the deck; RHP zero z ~ 1/(Cc*(1/gm2 - R0)),
            pushed to infinity when R0 = 1/gm2.
INSTANCES   XC0 (C0_W, C0_L), R0 (R0_W, R0_L), XMP3 (MP3_W, MP3_L -> gm2).
GAP         (A) measured 70.6228 deg -- 0.62 deg ABOVE the range top, i.e. the
            circuit as filed is OVER-COMPENSATED and marginally out of spec on
            the high side.
PLAUSIBILITY  PLAUSIBLE and FAVOURABLE. Being over-compensated means there is
            phase margin to SPEND, and the currency it buys is exactly UGBW.
            Shrinking Cc moves PM down toward the middle of the range and UGBW
            up toward its floor with one move. The keys are aligned, not opposed
            -- unusual, and it is why this spec set is reachable at all.
TENSION     R0 tracks 1/gm2. Any move to MP3_W or MP3_L changes gm2 and therefore
            DETUNES R0's zero cancellation silently -- PM will move for a reason
            that is not visible in the parameter that was changed. See
            circuit_decomposition.yaml cross_pattern_dependencies xpd1.
SIMULATE    Same .ac run, same saved node. PM = 180 + phase at the UGBW
            crossing, from the same registered extractor. None when UGBW is None.
SAVE ALSO   gm on XMP3 (to check R0 ~ 1/gm2 after any stage-2 move).

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
KEY 4 -- Power         CEILING 0.5813 mW    harder 0.63943 mW
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
DIRECTION   CEILING: met when sim <= 0.5813 mW. NOTE the harder bar is LARGER,
            not smaller -- a ceiling's margin is allowance, by design.
            THE REAL 0.5813 mW IS THE REQUIREMENT; the harder number is slack.
EXPRESSION  P = VDD * ( I_ref + I_tail + I_stage2 )
              I_ref    set by the XMP4-over-XMN4 diode stack (MP4_W dominates,
                       being the far smaller device: 2.3 um vs 236 um)
              I_tail   = (MN3_M / MN4_M) * I_ref     -> XMN3
              I_stage2 = (MN5_M / MN4_M) * I_ref     -> XMN5
            VDD = 1.8 V, fixed by the deck.
GAP         (A) measured 0.585366 mW -- 0.0041 mW OVER the real ceiling (+0.7%),
            though under the harder one. So Power starts marginally FAILING.
            (B) starts lower, since two mirror legs shrank at the seed.
PLAUSIBILITY  PLAUSIBLE but this is THE BINDING CONSTRAINT and the design's cost
            function. Gain and UGBW both want more gm; gm at fixed geometry is
            bought with current; current is this key. The whole run is a
            negotiation between key 4 and keys 1-2, mediated by the two levers
            that do NOT cost current: l (for Gain) and Cc (for UGBW).
LEVERS      MP4_W  -- coarsest of all; scales I_ref and hence EVERY branch
            MN4_W  -- the family's shared unit width; current density for all 3 legs
            MN4_M  -- the ratio DENOMINATOR: raising it lowers I_tail and
                      I_stage2 together without touching I_ref
            MN3_M / MN5_M -- per-leg, the only way to move tail and output
                      branch currents INDEPENDENTLY of each other
SIMULATE    `op` after the AC `write`, then `wrdata <file> i(vdd)`.
            Power_mW = |i(vdd)| * 1.8 * 1e3. Already present in the deck.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
OVER-CONSTRAINED? -- the cross-key judgement
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
NO KEY IS INDIVIDUALLY FLAGGED OVER-CONSTRAINED. The SET is tight and is flagged
MODERATELY CONSTRAINED, with the reason stated so a later shortfall can be
checked against it rather than re-derived:

  Gain must rise 2.43x AND UGBW must rise ~1.3-1.6x WHILE Power must FALL.
  Taken naively that is contradictory, because the textbook lever for both gain
  and bandwidth is more current.

  It is nevertheless reachable, and by exactly two current-free mechanisms:
    (i)  every device sits at l = 0.15 um, the process minimum, so gm/gds is at
         its floor everywhere. Length is free gain.
    (ii) PM starts 0.62 deg ABOVE its range, so Cc is oversized. Shrinking Cc is
         free bandwidth AND moves PM into range.
  If BOTH of those are exhausted -- gm/gds saturated at the l in force, and PM
  driven to the bottom of its range -- and a key is still short, then the target
  IS over-constrained on this topology, and the honest answer is a cascode (for
  Gain) or a re-spec, not more iterations. That is the pre-registered test for
  a STOP conclusion; it is written down here, before the loop runs, on purpose.

RISK CARRIED FORWARD (not a spec key, do not tune for it)
  There is no external Ibias. Power, and every gm downstream of it, is a
  nominal-1.8V/tt-corner number with no PTAT or bandgap behind it, so whatever
  Power margin the run achieves is NOT corner-proof. open_questions Q2.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
WHAT THE TESTBENCH MUST PROVIDE  (the contract step #4 audits against)
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  1. An `.ac` sweep wide enough to contain the 0 dB crossing (1 Hz..10 GHz: yes).
  2. `write <raw> v(vout)` inside `.control`, after `run`   -> Gain, UGBW, PM.
  3. An `op` analysis                                       -> Power.
  4. `wrdata <file> i(vdd)` after that `op`                 -> the Power number.
  5. Room for the op-probe splice: probe lines after `run`, the op/power save
     after the AC `write`.
  6. Conditions RECORDED, never assumed: VDD=1.8, vcm=0.9, cload=10p, tt.
     All four are set by the user's own deck. Nothing here may add one.

PLOT  YES -- this is a response-shaped spec (a gain, a bandwidth and a stability
      margin read off one curve). generate_plot_script.py at Step 3.
